-- CK-159: a derived `==` over a type whose payload's `eq` is specialised
-- (`H.eq : Holder Int, Holder Int -> Bool`) is accepted where it is used
-- at the specialised type. `W Int` holds a `Holder Int`, whose `eq` exists
-- and compares mod 10, so `W (Holder 3) 1 == W (Holder 13) 1` is `equal`.
-- At 8b98464 it is NOT EQUATABLE, with `absent_other`'s text ("a function
-- anywhere inside it") — there is no function, and no guarantee is at stake
-- (rule 7). Expected: it builds and prints `equal`. Oracle twin: the same
-- comparison through a tuple, `( Holder Int, Int )`, builds and prints
-- `equal`. (If the owner decides W Int stays refused, the fallback is a
-- `check/bad` whose message names `H.eq`; this fixture takes rule 7's side.)
pub type Holder a
    = Holder a


pub eq : Holder Int, Holder Int -> Bool
eq (Holder x) (Holder y) =
    modBy x 10 == modBy y 10
